1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,695
Theory becomes judgment when trucks,
legacy berth schemas, and on-call
rotations enter the room. This closing
episode is not a new framework feature.

2
00:00:09,815 --> 00:00:19,439
It is three condensed postmortems that
force you to pick from everything this
series taught — IoC and Boot, MVC and
data, security and cloud, tests,
observability,

3
00:00:19,559 --> 00:00:24,239
and architecture — and leave you with a
synthesis you can reuse on Monday.

4
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:32,280
### Case 1 — Gate latency without a
villain A harbor gate API met its error
SLO and missed its latency SLO.

5
00:00:32,399 --> 00:00:46,799
The first proposal was “move gate to
WebFlux.” Grafana showed HTTP p99 high;
the harbor.gate.release.duration timer was
elevated; OpenTelemetry showed ten
sequential billing quote spans per multi-
container truck.

6
00:00:46,920 --> 00:01:00,167
The fix was a bulk quote endpoint and a
single span — evidence from the
performance episode, not a programming-
model fashion. Heap and GC were innocent.
Architecture diagrams did not need
redrawing.

7
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:07,512
Takeaway: instrument business hops, read
the trace before changing stacks, optimize
the algorithm the spans reveal.

8
00:01:07,631 --> 00:01:21,863
### Case 2 — The god service that could
not test release A monolith package named
service imported web DTOs, JPA entities,
and the billing Feign client into one
class. Unit tests booted half of Spring.

9
00:01:21,983 --> 00:01:34,439
The team carved a hexagonal slice around
gate release: ReleaseGateUseCase in the
core, MVC and Feign adapters outside,
ArchUnit guarding imports. They did not
rewrite every admin CRUD screen.

10
00:01:34,559 --> 00:01:41,135
Release rules became testable with a fake
BillingPort and DutyProvider; production
wiring stayed Boot’s job.

11
00:01:41,255 --> 00:01:49,583
Takeaway: ports and adapters where change
and risk concentrate; layered pragmatism
elsewhere; dependency direction over
folder cosmetics.

12
00:01:49,703 --> 00:02:01,607
### Case 3 — Schedule board choking the
write model Operator boards joined berth
reservations, vessel names, and billing
state live against normalized write tables
under row locks from reserve.

13
00:02:01,727 --> 00:02:15,119
The team introduced mild CQRS:
schedule_board_view updated after commit
from BerthReserved, query controller
separate from command controller. They
added a Prometheus gauge on projection
lag.

14
00:02:15,239 --> 00:02:21,239
When lag spiked, on-call checked the
projector before blaming Postgres primary
CPU alone.

15
00:02:21,359 --> 00:02:29,735
Takeaway: separate read models when
screens and invariants diverge; emit
domain events you can project; observe the
projection itself.

16
00:02:29,855 --> 00:02:42,647
### Synthesis — a working order of attack
When a Spring harbor system hurts, walk a
deliberate path instead of grabbing a
buzzword: 1. See — Actuator, Micrometer,
Prometheus, Grafana, traces.

17
00:02:42,768 --> 00:02:55,560
If you cannot see it, you cannot rank it.
2. Stabilize — readiness probes, timeouts,
pools, memory bounds, graceful shutdown.
Survive the week. 3.

18
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:03,575
Simplify the hot path — fix N+1, batch
chatty Feign calls, cache only with hit
metrics. 4.

19
00:03:03,695 --> 00:03:14,280
Draw boundaries — layers first; hex/clean
rings where frameworks invade the core;
DDD where language and invariants are rich
(Berth, gate release). 5.

20
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:27,792
Decouple in time — BerthReserved and
outbox when sync fan-out couples
availability. 6. Split read/write — CQRS
when one model cannot serve both honesty
and the schedule board. 7.

21
00:03:27,912 --> 00:03:39,264
Prove — JUnit and Mockito at the tariff
core, slices for gate controllers,
Testcontainers for VesselRepository,
contracts for gate↔billing, dashboards and
alerts as part of the change.

22
00:03:39,384 --> 00:03:50,304
Spring’s through-line across the catalog
is unchanged from the opening lesson: keep
business logic in POJOs you can reason
about; let the container and ecosystem
handle assembly, infrastructure,

23
00:03:50,424 --> 00:03:52,944
and cross-cutting concerns.

24
00:03:53,063 --> 00:04:05,783
Boot accelerates the outer rings. Cloud
patterns and messaging address multi-
service quay reality. Observability closes
the feedback loop so architecture debates
meet production numbers.

25
00:04:05,903 --> 00:04:20,783
### What you should be able to do now
Explain why a bean is created and where a
request enters DispatcherServlet. Choose
JPA fetch strategies with SQL evidence.
Secure a resource with the filter chain
you can narrate.

26
00:04:20,903 --> 00:04:33,024
Test slices without booting the world.
Scrape gate meters, find a slow span on
check-in, and decide whether the next move
is a pool change, a port/adapter carve-
out, or a schedule-board read model.

27
00:04:33,144 --> 00:04:42,288
That combination — mechanism plus judgment
— is the series’ real deliverable. There
is no next handbook episode waiting behind
a curtain.

28
00:04:42,408 --> 00:04:53,016
The next lesson is the system you own:
pick one bottleneck on your quay, name the
Spring mechanism that addresses it,
measure before and after, and keep the
dependency arrows pointing toward the
domain.

29
00:04:53,136 --> 00:04:56,472
The handbook ends here; the work does not.
